          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Area"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.09,
            0.332,
            0.113
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Parallelogram"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.121,
            0.846,
            0.161
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can derive a special formula for the area of a parallelogram by converting it into a rectangle of equal area."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.17,
            0.844,
            0.214
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Give a method to convert a parallelogram into a rectangle of equal area. You can try this using a cut-out of a parallelogram."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.214,
            0.845,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Construct AX perpendicular to CD — represented in short as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "AX  \\perp  CD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " We call this a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "height",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the parallelogram. Cut the parallelogram into ∆AXD and trapezium ABCX."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.273,
            0.459,
            0.386
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.225,
                0.273,
                0.459,
                0.386
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.508,
            0.273,
            0.772,
            0.419
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.508,
                0.273,
                0.772,
                0.419
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.424,
            0.846,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Can ∆AXD and ABCX fit together, as shown in the figure, to get a rectangle?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.45,
            0.845,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "One simple way to check this is to identify the triangle that can complete ABCX to a rectangle, and then check if this triangle is congruent to ∆AXD."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.314,
            0.493,
            0.668,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.314,
                0.493,
                0.668,
                0.61
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.616,
            0.847,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle X = 90^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , and so "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle A = 90^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , since AB‖XC. We need another right angle to get a rectangle (what about the fourth angle?). To get the third right angle, extend XC to the right and then construct a line perpendicular to XC that passes through B. The ∆BYC completes ABCX to a rectangle. Is ∆AXD congruent to it?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.335,
            0.713,
            0.676,
            0.732
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "BY = AX (since ABYX is a rectangle)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.337,
            0.732,
            0.528,
            0.749
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_12.jpg",
          "content": "\\angle BTC = \\angle AAD = 90^{\\circ}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.335,
            0.75,
            0.726,
            0.769
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_13.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{BC} = \\mathrm{AD} (  since ABCD is a parallellogram  )"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.773,
            0.847,
            0.83
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, by the RHS congruency criterion, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta \\mathrm{BYC} \\cong \\Delta \\mathrm{AXD}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Thus, ∆AXD will fit exactly over the region occupied by ∆BYC, and convert the parallelogram into a rectangle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.834,
            0.846,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The process of cutting a figure into pieces and rearranging them to get a different figure of equal area is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "dissection.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
